 Because of the beta blocker, someone has gone in and cut the wires to the alarm sirens.

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:55.520
 So the patient has no idea they're in danger.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:56.880
 Right. The symptoms are gone.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:01.840
 But worse than that, the beta blocker also cut the water supply to the emergency sprinkler system,

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:02.320
 the liver.

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:03.600
 Oh, wow.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:06.400
 The fire is burning, the sprinklers are disabled,

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:08.320
 and the alarms are completely silent.

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.560
 It is the perfect physiological storm.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.680
 The patient is hurtling toward a hypoglycemic coma

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:17.040
 without a single physical indicator that anything is wrong.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:20.960
 It underscores why nurses and doctors must be hyper-vigilant

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.000
 when prescribing these specific blockers to diabetic patients.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:27.840
 It's just incredible how pulling one lever to fix a heart problem

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.800
 completely sabotages the liver's survival mechanics.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:34.240
 And this interplay isn't limited to beta blockers.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:36.480
 The lecture also covers alpha blockers.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:38.800
 Right. We established earlier that alpha receptors

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.600
 are primarily in the smooth muscles of the blood vessels.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:42.560
 The ones that clamp down.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:45.040
 Exactly. But they are also found in the smooth muscles

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:47.920
 of the urinary tract and the gastrointestinal system.

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:50.560
 And the lecture highlights drugs like Tamsilosin,

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.200
 better known as flomax, and terazosin.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:55.280
 These are alpha blockers.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.920
 And their primary clinical use isn't for blood pressure,

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:00.480
 but for helping patients, usually older men,

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:04.720
 deal with benign prostatic hyperplasia or an enlarged prostate.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:07.840
 And the instructor clarifies a really common misconception here.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:11.360
 Flowmax does not shrink the prostate gland itself.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:12.080
 It doesn't.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:14.800
 No. Instead, by blocking the alpha receptors

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.320
 in the surrounding smooth muscle of the urethra and the bladder neck,

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.880
 it forces those tissues to deeply relax.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.800
 It widens the pipe so the patient can void their bladder without obstruction.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:28.160
 The instructor actually mentions they also use it off label

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:30.880
 to help patients pass kidney stones for the exact same reason.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:33.600
 Right. Relaxing the ureters to let the stone drop.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.240
 But here is where it connects back to the blood vessels

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:39.680
 and why you listening needs to pay attention to systemic side effects.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.720
 If you are blocking alpha receptors to relax the bladder,

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:46.960
 you are inadvertently blocking alpha receptors in the blood vessels as well.

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:50.160
 Which introduces a massive mechanical fall risk.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:54.000
 Think about what happens in your body when you are lying down

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:55.520
 and you suddenly stand up.

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:56.880
 Okay. Gravity takes over.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:01.120
 Exactly. Gravity immediately tries to pull all your blood down into your legs.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:04.160
 If that happened unchecked, your brain would lose oxygen

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.320
 and you would instantly pass out.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:10.080
 But normally your sympathetic nervous system is constantly monitoring this.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.440
 The second you stand, your alpha receptors trigger instant

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.440
 vasoconstriction in your lower extremities.

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:15.680
 Right.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:20.720
 They squeeze the vessels to fight gravity and push the blood back up to your brain.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:22.000
 And it happens in milliseconds.

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:26.080
 But if you are taking Flowmax for your prostate,

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.600
 a significant portion of those alpha receptors are blocked by blank keys.

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:33.280
 You stand up, gravity pulls the blood down,

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:36.080
 the nervous system sends the signal to constrict the vessels,

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:37.680
 but the vessels can't respond.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:38.880
 Because they are blocked.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:41.520
 Exactly. The blood just pulls in your legs.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:43.520
 Your blood pressure completely bottoms out.

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:45.920
 It's called orthostatic hypotension.

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:48.880
 The patient gets incredibly dizzy, their vision blacks out,

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:51.600
 or they suffer a complete syncable episode.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:52.080
 They faint.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:56.000
 It perfectly illustrates the muddy waters of pharmacology.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:59.840
 You take a pill to help you pee, and the chemical consequence

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:04.960
 is that the invisible lever keeping you conscious when you stand up has been disconnected.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.880
 Right. It's all connected.

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:10.320
 But up to this point, we've only been talking about one side of the nervous system.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.280
 We've mapped out the sympathetic fight or flight accelerator,

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:15.600
 and we've mapped out the blockers that act as the brakes.

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:19.120
 But biologically, the absence of an accelerator

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:21.840
 does not automatically mean the body is resting.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:22.640
 Exactly.

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:26.000
 The human body does not rely on a single system to do everything.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:30.240
 It has an entirely separate, dedicated, hardwired neural network design,

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:35.440
 specifically for slowing down, recovering, and processing nutrients.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.800
 We are crossing over into the parasympathetic nervous system.

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:41.120
 The rest and digest lever.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:42.800
 Love the rest and digest.

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:45.600
 To manipulate this side of the equation,

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:49.200
 medical professionals use a class of drugs called colinergics,

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.360
 or parasympathome medics.

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:54.560
 While the sympathetic system relies on adrenaline,

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:59.200
 this parasympathetic system operates on a completely different chemical language.

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:02.080
 Its primary neurotransmitter is acetylcholine.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:03.040
 Acetylcholine?

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:03.680
 Yes.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:07.600
 Colonergic drugs are synthetic molecules designed to mimic acetylcholine

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:09.760
 and bind to its specific receptors.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:14.560
 And the physiological effects are the literal mirror image of an adrenaline rush.

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:18.400
 The lecture notes that when colonergics activate this system,

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:23.280
 they stimulate the intestines to aggressively increase bowel motility.

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:24.320
 They get things moving.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:24.960
 Right.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.040
 They contract the bladder to force urination.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.560
 They massively increase bodily secretions,

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:32.880
 salivation, sweating, gastric juices.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:37.840
 And they actively suppress the electrical signals in the heart to drop the heart rate.

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:42.400
 The instructor grounds this in a very relatable everyday experience.

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:47.040
 Think about the physiological state your body enters after you eat a massive heavy lunch.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:48.160
 The classic food coma.

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:50.080
 The classic food coma you feel sluggish.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:51.360
 Your eyelids get heavy.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.800
 You just want to sink into the couch.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:56.320
 And doctors use synthetic versions of this chemical too,

00:23:56.320 --> 00:23:59.680
 artificially force the body into this hyper-lubricated,

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:03.520
 hyper-motile state to treat very specific mechanical failures.

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:07.200
 The lecture brings up a fascinating disease called myasthenia gravis.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:11.760
 Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune neuromuscular disease.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:16.720
 The patient's own immune system attacks and destroys the acetylcholine receptors

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:18.480
 at the neuromuscular junction.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.360
 Which is what?

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:23.040
 It's the exact spot where nerves tell muscles to move.

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:25.040
 Because the signals can't get through,

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:28.640
 the patient experiences profound skeletal muscle weakness.

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:32.000
 They suffer from extreme stiffness, difficulty moving,

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:33.840
 and a rigid tightening of the joints.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:38.480
 The primary treatment for this is a colonergic drug called pyreidostigmy.

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:42.000
 And the instructor brings up an analogy from another educator

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:44.240
 that perfectly visualizes what this drug does.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.760
 They compare the patient to the tin man in the Wizard of Oz.

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:50.960
 It's a highly effective way to visualize the mechanism.

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:54.080
 Right, but listening to the cellular breakdown,

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:56.240
 it sounds less like general muscle weakness,

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:59.760
 and more like an engine that is completely seized up because it ran out of oil.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:01.040
 Yeah, the gears are locked.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:05.440
 The colonergic drug acts exactly like a biological WD-40.

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:08.880
 Because it artificially mimics acetylcholine,

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:11.520
 it forces the remaining receptors to fire constantly.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:15.840
 It massively increases all those bodily secretions and fluids we talked about.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:19.200
 It lubricates the entire physiological system,

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:22.960
 loosening the locked gears so the electrical signals can finally pass through,

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.800
 and the patient can move their limbs again.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:27.520
 It's a beautiful application of pharmacology.

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:32.080
 Using moisture and increased transmission to solve a rigid mechanical failure.

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:34.960
 But just as we saw with the synthetic adrenaline,

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:38.880
 there is a severe dark side when the specific lever is pulled too hard.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:44.640
 What happens when the rest and digest system goes into complete overdrive?

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:48.960
 The lecture calls it a colonergic crisis.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:53.520
 It is a severe overdose of parasympathetic stimulation.

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.560
 The instructor does not mince words here.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:02.160
 They describe a colonergic crisis as an exceptionally bad day for everyone involved.

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:06.080
 To train nurses to immediately recognize this crisis,

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:11.280
 they use a highly evocative and frankly horrifying mnemonic, sludge.

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:17.440
 Let's walk through sludge because breaking down each letter really paints a vivid picture

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:21.440
 of a body completely losing control of its internal fluid management.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:23.200
 All right, let's do it.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.320
 The S stands for salivation.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:28.880
 The patient begins to drool uncontrollably.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:32.480
 The salivary glands are just stuck in the on position.

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:33.440
 That sounds awful.

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:37.280
 It gets worse. The L is for lacrimation.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:41.040
 The tear ducts are hyperstimulated, so the patient is pouring tears down their face

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:44.160
 as if they are sobbing, but it's a purely chemical reaction.

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.680
 Wow, and the U.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:48.160
 U is urination.

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.680
 The smooth muscles of the bladder are forced into continuous contraction,

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:55.040
 resulting in a complete and total loss of bladder control.

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:56.160
 I'm seeing a theme here.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:57.920
 Fluids everywhere.

00:26:58.640 --> 00:26:59.120
 What's D?

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:01.520
 The D is defecation.

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:04.320
 The exact same mechanism hits the lower GI tract.

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:06.880
 Uncontrollable liquid bowel movements.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.280
 Which leads right into the G GI cramping.

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:12.560
 The intestines are spasming so violently as they try to process

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:17.360
 non-existent food that the patient is in agonizing abdominal pain.

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:20.000
 And finally, E for emesis.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:25.360
 Severe violent vomiting as the stomach violently expels its contents.

00:27:25.360 --> 00:27:26.960
 The instructor bluntly tells the students,

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:30.800
 "You've got bodily fluids coming out of every hole and you're stuck in the bathroom."

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:35.120
 The patient's biological plumbing has completely ruptured.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:36.720
 It's a nightmare.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:41.040
 But the danger here isn't just that it's messy or undignified.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:44.880
 The lecture explains that a colon-orgic crisis is rapidly lethal

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:48.160
 because of what all that fluid loss does to the blood chemistry.

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:51.600
 Right, it causes a catastrophic acid-base imbalance.

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:54.720
 The body operates in a very narrow pH window.

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:57.200
 When a patient is vomiting uncontrollably,

00:27:57.200 --> 00:28:01.280
 they are expelling massive amounts of highly acidic stomach acid.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:04.640
 And when you lose acid, your blood chemistry swings the other way.

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:06.560
 The patient becomes alkalotic.

00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:08.400
 The blood becomes too basic.

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:09.840
 But the crisis doesn't stop there.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:13.680
 As the patient continues to violently leak fluid from both ends

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:17.440
 through diarrhea and vomiting, their vascular volume plummets.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.480
 They become profoundly dehydrated at the cellular level.

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:22.800
 And when cells don't have enough fluid

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:26.800
 and oxygen to perform normal aerobic metabolism, they panic.

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:29.600
 Yes, and they switch to anaerobic metabolism.

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.960
 And the byproduct of anaerobic metabolism is lactic acid.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:33.920
 Exactly.

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:36.400
 So the patient's blood chemistry wildly swings

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:39.520
 from being dangerously alkalotic due to vomiting.

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:44.320
 To becoming severely acidotic, as lactic acid floods the dehydrated tissues,

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:46.800
 it is a chaotic lethal pendulum.

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:48.240
 And remember, this is all happening

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:49.920
 while the parasympathetic nervous system

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:52.880
 is simultaneously following its other prime directives,

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.600
 slowing the heart and constricting the airways.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:57.360
 Wait, constricting the airways too?

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:00.960
 Yes. So you have a patient who is violently leaking fluid.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:02.480
 Their blood is turning to acid.

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.200
 Their heart rate is dropping into fatal bradycardia.

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:07.760
 And their lung bronchials are shrinking so they can't breathe.

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:13.760
 It is a total systemic collapse, masquerading, as rest and digest.

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:14.640
 Exactly.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.200
 The initial treatment for this is a drug called atropine,

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:20.480
 which tries to block the receptors and reverse the fluid loss.

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:23.760
 But the instructor notes that if the crisis reaches the late stages,

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:26.720
 the medical team has to abandon the fluid problem entirely

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:30.000
 and hit the patient with epinephrine, pure adrenaline.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.200
 Just yank the other lever.

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:34.320
 Right. They have to violently yank the sympathetic lever

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.040
 to restart the heart and force the airways open,

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:39.520
 initiating a chemical tug of war for the patient's life.

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:42.720
 Which perfectly transitions us to the exact opposite problem.

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:46.480
 If colonergics open the biological floodgates,

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.760
 what do doctors use when a patient is essentially drowning

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:50.880
 in their own secretions?

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:53.040
 We turn to the anti-colonergic drugs.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:55.200
 These are competitive antagonists,

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:58.080
 just like the beta blockers occupied the adrenaline receptors.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:02.000
 Anti-colonergics physically occupy the acetylcholine receptors.

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:05.200
 They forcibly shut down the parasympathetic nervous system.

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:08.000
 And if the mnemonic for colonergics was sludge,

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:11.440
 the mnemonic the instructor uses for anti-colonergics

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:13.600
 is equally memorable if a bit crude.

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:16.240
 They tell the nurses to remember the effects as

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:20.080
 can't see, can't pee, can't spit, can't shit.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:24.640
 It perfectly encapsulates the extreme systemic drying

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:26.000
 effect these drugs create.

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:27.840
 By blocking the rest and digest signals,

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:29.360
 you halt all fluid production

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.680
 and paralyze the smooth muscle contractions.

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:34.720
 Breaking that down, can't see because the drug

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.800
 dries out the lubricating fluid in the eyes

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.680
 and forces the pupils to dilate, causing blurry vision.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:43.440
 Can't pee because it paralyzes the bladder muscles,

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:44.880
 causing urinary retention.

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:49.600
 Can't spit because it completely shuts off the salivary glands,

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:53.440
 creating a severe case of dry mouth and can't shit.

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:56.160
 Because it freezes the motility of the intestines,

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:58.080
 leading to severe constipation.

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:01.040
 It sounds miserable, but these extreme drying

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:04.160
 and paralyzing effects are incredibly useful clinically,

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:06.160
 when they are targeted correctly.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:08.720
 The lecture covers several specific applications.

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:10.880
 Take a drug like oxybutinin.

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:13.200
 The instructor notes that oxybutinin is prescribed

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.160
 for overactive bladders or stressing continents.

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:19.680
 If a patient is leaking urine every time they laugh or sneeze,

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:22.080
 their bladder muscles are too sensitive.

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:23.520
 They are spasming.

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:25.680
 By giving it anti-colonergic,

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:28.560
 you partially paralyze those specific smooth muscles.

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:31.440
 It decreases the frequency of the spasms

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:32.800
 and dries up the problem.

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:35.200
 Or consider the drug bental,

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:39.040
 which is heavily used for irritable bowel syndrome or IBS.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:42.400
 IBS is characterized by painful intestinal spasms

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:43.840
 and hyper-motility.

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:45.360
 The gut is moving too fast.

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:48.960
 Bental acts as a targeted anti-spasmodic.

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:50.720
 It freezes the gut's motion,

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:53.280
 stopping the severe cramping and halting the diarrhea.

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:56.240
 The lecture also mentions scapolamine.

00:31:56.240 --> 00:31:57.680
 Have you ever been on a cruise ship

00:31:57.680 --> 00:32:00.800
 and seen people with little circular patches behind their ears?

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:02.000
 That is scapolamine.

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:05.440
 Motion sickness is deeply tied to the parasympathetic system.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.520
 When you are on a rocking boat,

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:09.760
 the fluid in your inner ear shifts violently.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:12.560
 It sends conflicting signals to your brain.

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:14.560
 Your eyes say you are standing still,

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:16.560
 but your inner ear says you are moving.

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:18.480
 And the brain thinks you're poisoned.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:21.600
 Exactly. The brain interprets this confusion as poisoning

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.840
 and triggers intense nausea and vomiting.

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:26.880
 Scapolamine is a potent anti-colonergic.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.440
 The patch delivers the drug continuously,

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:32.480
 which physically dries up the excess fluid in the inner ear

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:34.160
 and crosses the blood brain barrier

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:36.640
 to chemically block the nausea receptors

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:38.480
 in the vomiting center of the brain.

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:39.920
 It's a lifesaver on boats.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.240
 But as I was going through the source material,

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:44.480
 I hit a specific anti-colonergic drug

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:45.600
 that made me stop reading

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:48.720
 and really think about the emotional weight of this profession.

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:50.880
 The drug is called robinol.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:53.600
 Ah, yes, robinol.

00:32:53.600 --> 00:32:56.000
 The instructor notes that robinol is primarily used

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.400
 in end-of-life care, in hospice situations,

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:01.360
 to treat a specific physiological phenomenon

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:03.120
 known as the death rattle.

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:03.440
 Yes.

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:07.920
 The death rattle is an incredibly distinct agonizing sound.

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:11.520
 As a dying patient enters the final stages of life,

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:14.880
 they lose consciousness and lose the autonomic reflex to swallow.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:15.680
 Okay.

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:18.640
 However, their salivary glands often continue to produce fluid.

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:22.160
 That saliva pools in the back of the throat and the upper airway.

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:25.200
 As the unconscious patient draws their final breaths

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:26.400
 through that pooled fluid,

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:29.680
 it creates a loud, wet, gurgling rattle.

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:32.960
 The instructor explains that nurses administer robinol,

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:34.560
 this intense drying agent,

00:33:35.120 --> 00:33:37.120
 to instantly shut down the salivary glands

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:38.320
 and silence the rattle.

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:39.600
 But reading this,

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:41.760
 I found myself grappling with a really sensitive question.

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:42.880
 I want to know your take on this.

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:44.880
 Okay.

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:46.480
 If the patient is already actively dying,

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:49.680
 if their brain is shutting down and they are losing consciousness,

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:53.280
 is this intense chemical intervention actually doing anything

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:55.120
 to make it easier for the patient to breathe?

00:33:55.760 --> 00:33:58.400
 Or is this drug being administered purely to make

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:00.480
 the grieving family sitting around the bed

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:03.920
 feel less disturbed by the horrifying sound?

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:05.120
 It's a profound question,

00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:07.600
 and it cuts straight to the heart of the ethical complexities

00:34:07.600 --> 00:34:08.880
 and palliative care.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:12.080
 And the source text actually validates your exact suspicion.

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:12.640
 Really?

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:12.880
 Yes.

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:16.000
 The instructor explicitly tells the nursing class

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:19.600
 that while the death rattle is deeply traumatic to listen to,

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:22.640
 clinical evidence suggests it does not cause physical harm,

00:34:22.640 --> 00:34:24.960
 suffocation, or distress to the dying patient.

00:34:25.680 --> 00:34:29.120
 The patient is almost universally entirely unaware of it.

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:30.080
 Wow.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:32.720
 So the patient isn't suffering from the fluid.

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:33.120
 They aren't.

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:36.160
 The instructor confirms that robinol is administered

00:34:36.160 --> 00:34:39.200
 in these specific end-of-life situations largely,

00:34:39.200 --> 00:34:42.320
 and sometimes entirely for the psychological comfort

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:44.000
 of the family members in the room.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:45.520
 That makes total sense, though.

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:46.400
 It does.

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:48.160
 When a family hears that groogling sound,

00:34:48.160 --> 00:34:50.560
 their immediate visceral reaction is panic.

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:53.440
 They believe their loved one is actively choking

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:54.880
 to death in front of them.

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:58.160
 It turns a peaceful passing into a traumatic memory.

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:01.520
 So the medical team utilizes a powerful anti-colonergic drug

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:04.480
 to chemically dry the patient's airway out,

00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:06.400
 not to save the biological machine,

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:10.800
 but to provide psychological peace to the survivors.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:15.760
 It highlights such a deeply human emotional side of pharmacology.

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:19.680
 The chemical lever is pulled to save the memory of a peaceful death.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:22.320
 It is one of the heaviest realities of nursing.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:25.680
 You are treating the room, not just the patient in the bed.

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:28.080
 Which is a beautiful, if heavy transition

00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:29.920
 into the final frontier of our deep dive.

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:32.160
 Up to this point, we have been exploring

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:36.080
 how medical professionals tweak the body's dials up and down.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:39.440
 A little more adrenaline here, a little less acycline there,

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.880
 tuning an engine that is still actively running.

00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:43.280
 Right.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:47.040
 But now we're looking at total system override.

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:50.480
 We're exploring the intense, highly controlled world

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:52.480
 of anesthesia and paralytics.

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:55.040
 What happens when the medical team needs to take

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:57.360
 the central nervous system completely offline?

00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:02.880
 The lecture establishes the baseline by breaking anesthesia into three tiers.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:07.200
 You have general anesthesia, which is medically induced total and consciousness.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:10.480
 You have local anesthesia, which is selectively numbing

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:13.280
 a specific physical area while the patient remains awake.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:18.480
 And you have moderate anesthesia, frequently called conscious sedation.

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:20.720
 And the drugs they use to achieve these states

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:23.760
 rely on some incredibly complex biological mechanisms.

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:28.160
 Let's look at conscious sedation first, with a drug called presidex.

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:31.200
 The instructor framed its use in a way that anyone

00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:35.680
 who has worked in customer service, let alone a hospital, can instantly relate to.

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:37.040
 Oh, the ER scenario?

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:37.360
 Yeah.

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:40.480
 Imagine you were working the night shift in the ER.

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:43.040
 A patient is brought in who is highly intoxicated,

00:36:43.040 --> 00:36:45.600
 or perhaps high on synthetic stimulants.

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:47.280
 They are out of their mind.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:51.920
 They are aggressive, combative, swinging at the nurses and completely uncooperative.

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:55.520
 The instructor jokes that nursing is the only profession,

00:36:55.520 --> 00:36:57.600
 or if someone is screaming at you and trying to hit you,

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:00.800
 you can legally just knock them unconscious and calmly say,

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:02.560
 "I'll try talking to you again tomorrow morning."

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:05.840
 And their primary weapon for that is presidex.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:09.680
 But here is the biological twist that threw me for a loop.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:14.240
 The instructor notes that presidex is actually an alpha-adrenergic agonist.

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:14.960
 Right.

00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:15.760
 Wait a minute.

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:18.000
 We just spent the first 20 minutes of this deep dive,

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:21.600
 establishing that alpha agonists, like Levafed and adrenaline,

00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:23.600
 are the ultimate fight-or-flight accelerators.

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:28.800
 How can an accelerator drug chemically chill out a raging combat of patient?

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:33.040
 This is where we have to drill down into the microscopic subtypes of these receptors.

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:34.880
 Not all alpha receptors are the same.

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:36.080
 Okay, lay it on me.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:40.800
 Presidex does not target the alpha-1 receptors in the blood vessels that cause constriction.

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:45.840
 It specifically targets a highly specialized receptor in the brain stem called the alpha-2 receptor.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:46.560
 Alpha-2.

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:47.280
 Yes.

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:52.000
 When a synthetic molecule binds to and stimulates the specific alpha-2 receptor,

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:54.080
 it doesn't trigger the release of adrenaline.

00:37:54.080 --> 00:37:56.400
 It triggers a negative feedback loop.

00:37:56.400 --> 00:38:00.400
 It chemically tricks the brain into thinking, we have far too much adrenaline in the system

00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:00.720
 right now.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.960
 We need to completely shut down production.

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:03.760
 Oh.

00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:07.120
 So it's an agonist that functions as an antagonist.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:11.760
 You stimulate the receptor to force the brain to shut off its own adrenaline supply.

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:12.800
 Precisely.

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:15.120
 By starving the brain of its endogenous adrenaline,

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:18.000
 the patient's severe anxiety evaporates.

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:20.960
 They enter a state of deep, calm sedation.

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:23.200
 But the absolute magic of presidex,

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:28.480
 and the reason ER doctors and ICU nurses rely on it so heavily is what it doesn't do.

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:29.760
 What doesn't it do?

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:32.720
 Most heavy sedatives depress the central nervous system so much

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:35.280
 that the brain forgets to tell the lungs to breathe.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:38.560
 If you give too much, you have to insert a mechanical breathing tube.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:41.600
 Presidex does not suppress the respiratory drive.

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:44.880
 The patient can be chemically cooled off, sleeping peacefully,

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:47.680
 and they will continue breathing completely on their own.

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:50.080
 It is a brilliant physiological loophole.

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:51.200
 It really is.

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:55.520
 But when conscious sedation isn't enough, we cross into general anesthesia.

00:38:55.520 --> 00:38:59.520
 And the lecture immediately introduces the heavyweight champion of the operating room,

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:00.640
 propofol.

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:04.880
 The instructor starts this section by asking the class a grim pop culture trivia question.

00:39:05.600 --> 00:39:10.240
 Who is the most famous person we know who died from a propofol overdose?

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:12.640
 And of course, the answer is Michael Jackson.

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:13.200
 Exactly.

00:39:13.840 --> 00:39:16.480
 The instructor notes that patients are frequently terrified

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:20.800
 when they hear the name of the drug because of that highly publicized association.

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:22.720
 Its reputation precedes it.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:27.200
 But pharmacologically, propofol is fascinating because of its physical composition.

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:30.720
 The instructor describes it as a thick, milky white substance.

00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:33.040
 It doesn't look like normal clear IV fluids.

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:34.400
 Why is that?

00:39:34.960 --> 00:39:37.280
 Because propofol is a lipid emulsion.

00:39:37.280 --> 00:39:40.720
 The active anesthetic chemical is completely suspended in fat.

00:39:41.360 --> 00:39:46.400
 It is essentially pure liquid fat being pushed directly into a patient's veins.

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:50.880
 And that unique physical property creates a massive hidden metabolic risk

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:52.000
 that I had never considered.

00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:56.000
 When you push fat into the bloodstream to put someone to sleep,

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:58.800
 the body eventually has to process and clear that fat.

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:02.800
 And the organ responsible for processing fats is the liver.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:06.080
 The liver has to metabolize the propofol exactly the same way.

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:09.040
 It metabolizes dietary cholesterol from a cheeseburger.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:12.480
 So the instructor warns the nurses that if they are preparing to sedate a patient

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:17.200
 who has severe hyperlipidemia, dangerously high cholesterol,

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:22.880
 high triglycerides, or severe atherosclerosis, which is plaque buildup in their arteries,

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:25.200
 propofol could be uniquely dangerous.

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:30.320
 Because you are taking a liver that is already failing to process the fat in the patient's diet.

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:35.360
 And you are overwhelming it with a massive concentrated lipid load.

00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:38.480
 If the liver can't clear the lipid emulsion, it built up in the blood.

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:43.600
 It can worsen their arterial blockages, trigger severe pancreatitis,

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:45.680
 or cause acute liver dysfunction.

00:40:45.680 --> 00:40:49.200
 It is a stark reminder that the drug putting your brain to sleep

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:51.920
 is intimately interacting with your cholesterol levels.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:54.800
 The body is an entirely closed interconnected loop.

00:40:55.360 --> 00:40:57.920
 It really forces you to look at the whole picture.

00:40:57.920 --> 00:41:03.040
 Speaking of interconnected loops, the lecture moves into local anesthetics,

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:08.800
 specifically lidocaine, and reveals what I think is the most ingenious medical trick

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:10.240
 in the entire transcript.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:11.760
 Oh, this is a great one.

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:14.720
 Think about a surgeon performing a delicate facial surgery.

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:19.120
 Maybe they are removing a complex skin cancer mole from a cheek,

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:21.520
 or performing reconstructive surgery on the nose.

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:25.120
 The human face is incredibly vascularized.

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:28.080
 It is packed with thousands of tiny blood vessels.

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:32.800
 It bleeds profusely, and the blood cycles through the tissue incredibly fast.

00:41:32.800 --> 00:41:35.920
 Which presents a major pharmacological problem.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:40.480
 If a surgeon injects lidocaine into the cheek to numb the nerves around the mole,

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:43.840
 the rapid blood flow in the face simply washes the lidocaine away.

00:41:43.840 --> 00:41:44.240
 Right.

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:47.200
 The drug gets absorbed into the general bloodstream and carried off

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:48.480
 before he can finish the job.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:51.760
 And the cheek regains feeling in a matter of minutes.

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:55.680
 The numbing effect won't last long enough for the surgeon to actually complete the procedure.

00:41:55.680 --> 00:41:59.040
 So how do they alter the local chemistry to force the drug to stay put?

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:02.160
 They mix the lidocaine with epinephrine.

00:42:02.160 --> 00:42:05.520
 They combine the numbing agent with pure adrenaline.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:10.800
 They inject a highly concentrated dose of the ultimate fight or flight accelerator

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:13.120
 right alongside the localized nerve block.

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:16.160
 Because, as we learned in the very first segment,

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:21.280
 epinephrine hitting alpha receptors causes instant, violent phasochis friction.

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:27.520
 It clamps all the blood vessels in that specific one inch square of the cheek completely shut.

00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:31.520
 The blood flow to that tiny area halts entirely.

00:42:32.080 --> 00:42:36.320
 And by clamping the surrounding vessels shut, the epinephrine builds a chemical dam.

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:38.720
 The blood can't rush in to wash the lidocaine away.

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:42.880
 The numbing agent is trapped exactly where the surgeon injected it,

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:46.480
 drastically extending the lifespan and effectiveness of the anesthetic.

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:52.400
 It is absolute biological genius using an accelerator to trap a blocker.

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:56.000
 And as a crazy, almost unbelievable side note,

00:42:56.560 --> 00:43:00.320
 the instructor casually drops a wild piece of medical trivia here.

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:03.840
 They mentioned that in very specific facial and sinus surgeries,

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:08.080
 where severe bleeding is a massive risk, there is another drug they can use

00:43:08.080 --> 00:43:13.440
 that naturally acts as both an intense local anesthetic and a violent vasoconstrictor.

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:15.920
 Yes, medical grade cocaine.

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:16.640
 Cocaine.

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:22.720
 Yes, cocaine's pharmacological profile is unique because it profoundly numbs mucosal tissues,

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:26.320
 while simultaneously shrinking the blood vessels to stop bleeding.

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:30.080
 The instructor notes that for certain delicate ear, nose, and throat surgeries,

00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:32.480
 they use legal pharmaceutical cocaine powder.

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:33.920
 That is just wild.

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:37.840
 They even mentioned that you will occasionally see it written plainly on the surgical chart.

00:43:37.840 --> 00:43:40.240
 Patient has been given legal cocaine.

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:42.320
 The things you learn in an advanced nursing class.

00:43:42.880 --> 00:43:47.360
 But as wild as medical cocaine is, we have to talk about what the instructor calls

00:43:47.360 --> 00:43:51.840
 the most mind-bending drug in their arsenal, the ketamine paradox.

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:55.680
 Ketamine stands entirely alone in the world of anesthesia.

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:58.640
 When doctors administer almost any other general anesthetic,

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:03.920
 like propofol or fentanyl, the drugs act as universal central nervous system depressants.

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:05.280
 Everything slows down.

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:08.800
 The heart rate drops, the blood pressure plummets, and most importantly,

00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:12.080
 the respiratory drive slows down or completely stops.

00:44:12.080 --> 00:44:14.960
 You usually have to intubate the patient and breathe for them.

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:16.960
 But ketamine breaks all those rules.

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:17.920
 It does.

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:22.320
 Ketamine maintains near-perfect cardiovascular and respiratory stability.

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:24.000
 It does not crash the blood pressure.

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.880
 It does not suppress the brain's command to the lungs to breathe.

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:30.160
 You can give a patient a massive anesthetic dose of ketamine,

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:35.040
 and their heart will keep a perfect rhythm and they will continue breathing room air on their own,

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:39.040
 all while being completely, totally disconnected from physical pain.

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:40.720
 Which begs the obvious question.

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:45.120
 If it perfectly blocks pain without threatening the heart or lungs,

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:48.160
 why isn't it the only anesthetic drug they ever use?

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:52.480
 Why bother with the dangerous lipid emotions of propofol?

00:44:52.480 --> 00:44:56.480
 Because the psychological side effects of ketamine are staggering.

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:59.040
 Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:02.000
 It causes intense psychomimetic effects.

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:06.800
 It induces profound, vivid, often terrifying hallucinations.

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:09.680
 The clinical slang for this is "the K-hole",

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:11.840
 which the instructor actually references.

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:16.800
 The clinical anecdotes the instructor shares about operating on patients under ketamine are surreal.

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:17.920
 Oh, they are bizarre.

00:45:17.920 --> 00:45:21.840
 Because the patient is breathing fine and their vitals are perfectly stable,

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:26.320
 doctors can perform intense bedside surgeries using only ketamine.

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:30.480
 The instructor describes a scenario where they are assisting in a bedside procedure.

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:34.560
 The doctor has their hands physically inside the patient's open abdomen.

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:37.360
 The physical trauma of the surgery is massive.

00:45:37.360 --> 00:45:38.720
 But the patient is awake.

00:45:39.280 --> 00:45:41.040
 Their eyes are open.

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:44.160
 Their brain is completely severed from the body's pain receptors,

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:45.440
 so they don't feel the scalpel.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:48.480
 But because of the dissociative hallucinations,

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:51.600
 their mind is firing on all cylinders in an alternate dimension.

00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:56.000
 The instructor notes the patient was cheerfully looking at the ICU nurses,

00:45:56.000 --> 00:45:58.640
 and enthusiastically inviting them to their future wedding,

00:45:59.440 --> 00:46:02.960
 completely oblivious to the fact that their abdomen was laid open.

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:06.720
 It is a surreal division of mind and body.

00:46:06.720 --> 00:46:08.880
 The biological machine is being cut into,

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:13.200
 but the consciousness driving the machine is wandering through a dreamscape.

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:14.720
 It is a staggering image.

00:46:15.520 --> 00:46:19.360
 But this concept of severing the mind from the body brings us to the final,

00:46:19.360 --> 00:46:24.560
 and undoubtedly the most serious medical law discussed in this entire source text.

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:28.480
 We have explored how to put the brain to sleep.

00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:33.120
 But what happens when the ICU team needs to stop the physical body entirely?

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:35.120
 We have to talk about paralytics.

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:39.200
 The clinical term is neuromuscular blocking agents.

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:43.040
 The specific drug heavily focused on in the lecture is succinylcholine.

00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:46.640
 These drugs do exactly what they sound like they do.

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:51.440
 They aggressively bind to the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction,

00:46:51.440 --> 00:46:53.040
 but they don't trigger movement.

00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:53.920
 They block it.

00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:55.920
 They permanently block the signal.

00:46:56.480 --> 00:47:00.880
 They plunge the patient into a state of total, flaccid paralysis.

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:04.160
 Every skeletal muscle in the body goes completely limp.

00:47:04.160 --> 00:47:07.280
 The lecture explains why this extreme intervention is necessary.

00:47:08.320 --> 00:47:12.880
 Often in the ICU, a critically ill patient will subconsciously fight the

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:15.680
 mechanical ventilator that is trying to breathe for them,

00:47:15.680 --> 00:47:16.960
 which damages their lungs.

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:21.520
 Or a patient will have uncontrollable, continuous seizures.

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:24.640
 If the brain is misfiring and won't stop seizing,

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:30.000
 you administer a paralytic to freeze the muscles so the physical body stops destroying itself.

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:34.000
 But there is a monumental lethal danger here.

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:39.200
 When we say it paralyzes every skeletal muscle, that includes the diaphragm.

00:47:39.200 --> 00:47:41.360
 The muscle that pulls air into your lungs.

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:41.680
 Yes.

00:47:42.240 --> 00:47:44.800
 The moment you push succinylcholine into an IV,

00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:48.880
 the patient instantly loses the physical ability to breathe.

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:50.240
 They will suffocate in minutes.

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:53.760
 You must immediately take over their airway with a machine.

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:58.000
 But more than the physical danger, the instructor emphasizes a psychological

00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:01.280
 terror that dictates the ultimate ethical rule of the ICU.

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:05.200
 Parallitics are strictly mechanical.

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:08.560
 They do absolutely nothing to the central nervous system.

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:09.600
 They do not numb pain.

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:11.920
 They do not alter consciousness.

00:48:11.920 --> 00:48:13.360
 They do not induce sleep.

00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:18.880
 If a nurse were to push a dose of succinylcholine into a patient without administering any other drugs,

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:20.800
 the patient would be fully awake.

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:23.760
 They would be fully aware of their surroundings.

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:26.000
 They would feel every ounce of physical pain.

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:29.280
 But they would be completely trapped inside a paralyzed shell,

00:48:29.920 --> 00:48:33.120
 unable to move a single millimeter, unable to open an eyelid,

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:36.400
 and unable to take a breath as they slowly suffocated.

00:48:36.960 --> 00:48:39.920
 It is the literal definition of the ultimate nightmare.

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:43.760
 It is locked in syndrome induced by a syringe.

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:48.240
 And the instructor drives home the unbreakable sacred ethical rule of critical care medicine.

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:54.240
 If you are going to paralyze a patient, you absolutely must chemically sedate them first.

00:48:55.120 --> 00:49:01.040
 You must push the propofol or the presidex to put the mind asleep before you freeze the body.

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:05.440
 To administer a paralytic without a sedative is considered a profound ethical violation.

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:09.200
 You must protect the consciousness before you shut down the machine.

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:14.480
 It is a sobering, heavy reminder of the immense power these professionals wield.

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:16.400
 They aren't just adjusting blood pressure.

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:20.240
 They hold the chemical levers that control consciousness, pain,

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:22.080
 and the boundary line between life and death.

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:26.160
 And that brings us to the end of this incredible journey through the source material.

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:29.520
 The muddy waters of the human nervous system.

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:31.920
 We have seen the ultimate accelerator of adrenaline,

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:34.640
 sacrificing tissue and toes to save a beating heart.

00:49:35.200 --> 00:49:37.360
 We've seen the breaks of beta blockers,

00:49:37.360 --> 00:49:41.360
 completely silencing the biological fire alarms of a crashing diabetic.

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:47.840
 We've watched the rest and digest system overflow into a lethal, acidotic, colonergic crisis.

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:51.280
 And we've seen the intense drying power of anticholinergics,

00:49:51.280 --> 00:49:54.160
 silencing a death rattle to comfort a grieving family.

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:59.600
 We have explored the razor-thin microscopic chemical line between resting,

00:49:59.600 --> 00:50:02.080
 fighting, and total unconsciousness.

00:50:02.080 --> 00:50:05.200
 Which leaves us with one final mind-bending thought to ponder.

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:09.440
 Towards the very end of the lecture, while the instructor is discussing the bizarre,

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:13.840
 dissociative effects of ketamine, they briefly mention a new frontier in medicine.

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:17.680
 Ketamine, and even synthetic stimulants like MDMA,

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:21.120
 are currently entering highly successful legal clinical trials.

00:50:21.760 --> 00:50:24.640
 But they aren't being used as anesthetics to numb physical pain.

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:28.800
 They are being used to treat severe, treatment-resistant anxiety,

00:50:29.360 --> 00:50:31.600
 deep depression, and profound PTSD.

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:37.840
 Researchers are utilizing highly controlled microdosis of these intense drugs to help trauma

00:50:37.840 --> 00:50:40.960
 victims process memories they otherwise couldn't face.

00:50:40.960 --> 00:50:44.560
 And building on everything we've unpacked today, I want to lead you with this question.

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:49.840
 For decades, modern medicine has viewed these intense drugs purely as physical tools.

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:54.000
 We use a paralytic like satsonylcholine to freeze a lung,

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.400
 so a mechanical ventilator can force air into it.

00:50:57.040 --> 00:51:01.120
 We use the alpha agonists of epinephrine, mixed with lidocaine,

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:05.200
 to build a chemical dam so a surgeon's scalpel can cut without the patient feeling it.

00:51:05.920 --> 00:51:10.640
 We use these profound chemical levers to force the physical body into compliance

00:51:10.640 --> 00:51:12.000
 so it can survive a trauma.

00:51:12.560 --> 00:51:13.680
 It's all physical.

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:19.120
 Exactly, but if these exact same chemicals, these profound dissociatives and synthetic

00:51:19.120 --> 00:51:24.800
 neurotransmitters, are now being meticulously microdosed to rewire depression and psychological

00:51:24.800 --> 00:51:30.160
 trauma, are we on the verge of treating the human mind with the same mechanical precision

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:31.680
 we use to treat the physical body?

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:33.680
 That is a wild thought.

00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:37.200
 If we can chemically paralyze a seizing muscle to allow it to reset,

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:41.200
 could these drugs be the key to temporarily paralyzing trauma?

00:51:42.000 --> 00:51:46.320
 Could we use a chemical lever to disconnect the brain from its psychological agony

00:51:46.320 --> 00:51:48.560
 just long enough for the mind to finally heal itself?

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:51.280
 That is a staggering implication.

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:56.240
 The idea that the brutal chemical precision of the intensive care unit

00:51:56.240 --> 00:52:00.160
 could ultimately become the architectural blueprint for healing the human psyche.

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:03.200
 It fundamentally changes how you look at a pill, doesn't it?

00:52:04.240 --> 00:52:06.720
 Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

00:52:06.720 --> 00:52:11.520
 Take a moment today to appreciate that invisible microscopic chemical symphony

00:52:11.520 --> 00:52:13.520
 happening inside your body right now.

00:52:13.520 --> 00:52:15.920
 The muddy waters of pharmacology are deep,

00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:19.280
 but they are an absolute marvel to behold once you know what to look for.

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:24.720
 We started today talking about how much we love the clean binary precision of an x-ray

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:25.680
 showing a broken bone.

00:52:26.400 --> 00:52:31.120
 But after diving into the chaotic world of adrenic receptors, negative feedback loops,

00:52:31.120 --> 00:52:35.360
 and chemical overrides, I think I prefer the muddy waters.

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:39.360
 Because in those invisible complex chemical levers,

00:52:39.360 --> 00:52:43.120
 we find the true staggering resilience of the human machine.

00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:47.040
 Until next time, keep diving deep.

